```typescript
import { Component, Directive, input } from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]',
  host: { '[style.backgroundColor]': 'appHighlight()' },
})
class HighlightDirective {
  readonly appHighlight = input('lemonchiffon');
}

@Component({
  imports: [HighlightDirective],
  template: `<p [appHighlight]="'aliceblue'">Reusable host behaviour</p>`,
})
class HelpMessage {}
```

Angular's built-in control-flow blocks decide which views exist. `@if` evaluates a condition and renders the matching branch. `@switch` compares one value with its cases using strict equality and renders the matching case. `@for` creates a view for each item in an iterable and exposes values such as `$index`, `$first`, and `$last`.

A loop also needs a `track` expression. It should identify the same logical item across inserts, deletes, and reorders, usually with a database ID. Angular uses that identity to match data items to existing DOM views. `track $index` is suitable only when the collection is effectively static, because positions change when items are inserted or moved.

For example:

```typescript
template: `
  @if (users.length) {
    @for (user of users; track user.id) { <p>{{ user.name }}</p> }
  } @else { <p>No users found.</p> }
`
```

Control flow changes view existence, so hidden branches do not keep every child component alive. Duplicate or unstable tracking keys can associate the wrong DOM state with an item. The aim is not to put business logic into a template; conditions should describe view state, and complicated calculations should remain in the class or derived state.

```typescript
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';

interface Task { id: number; title: string; done: boolean; }

@Component({
  selector: 'task-list',
  template: `
    @switch (status()) {
      @case ('loading') { <p>Loading tasks…</p> }
      @case ('ready') {
        @for (task of tasks(); track task.id) {
          <p>{{ task.title }} — {{ task.done ? 'done' : 'open' }}</p>
        } @empty { <p>No tasks yet.</p> }
      }
      @default { <p>Tasks could not be loaded.</p> }
    }
  `,
})
class TaskList {
  readonly status = signal<'loading' | 'ready' | 'error'>('ready');
  readonly tasks = signal<Task[]>([{ id: 1, title: 'Review bindings', done: false }]);
}
```

A custom attribute directive packages behaviour for an element that already exists. The class is decorated with `@Directive` and normally uses an attribute selector such as `[appHighlight]`. That means Angular creates the directive whenever the matching attribute appears in a compiled template.

The directive can expose an input and connect its state to the host through the `host` metadata. For example:

```typescript
@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]',
  host: { '[class.highlighted]': 'active()', '(focus)': 'active.set(true)' },
})
class HighlightDirective { active = signal(false); }
```

A component imports `HighlightDirective` and writes `<input appHighlight>`. When the input receives focus, the directive changes its signal and Angular applies the host class. A configurable directive can use an input alias so `[appHighlight]="color"` also supplies a value.

The boundary is important. A directive should enhance host behaviour while leaving the element's meaning intact. If the feature needs its own view, a component is usually better. If the requirement is only a static colour or hover rule, CSS is simpler. Event behaviour should also include keyboard and focus interaction where appropriate; a mouse-only directive can make an interface inaccessible. A useful attribute directive therefore has a focused selector, explicit inputs, predictable host effects, and no hidden application-level state.

```typescript
import { Component, Directive, input, signal } from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]',
  host: {
    '[style.outline]': "active() ? '2px solid ' + appHighlight() : 'none'",
    '(focus)': 'active.set(true)',
    '(blur)': 'active.set(false)',
  },
})
class HighlightDirective {
  readonly appHighlight = input('#2563eb');
  readonly active = signal(false);
}

@Component({
  imports: [HighlightDirective],
  template: `<label>Email <input [appHighlight]="'#15803d'"></label>`,
})
class ContactField {}
```
